==English==
{{was wotd|2012|March|23}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|escapade|t=the act of escaping; a trick}}, borrowed from {{der|en|osp|escapada}}, from {{m|osp|escapar|t=to escape}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*excappō|t=to escape}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|ĕs'kə-pād'}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɛskəˌpeɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-escapade.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[daring]] or [[adventurous]] act; an undertaking which goes against [[convention]].
#* {{RQ:Johnson History of the Pyrates|chapter=Of Captain [[w:Howell Davis|Howel Davis]], and His Crew|page=202|passage=The Manner of living among the ''Portugueze'' here is, with the utmost Frugality and Temperance. {{...}} The beſt of them (excepting the Governor now and then) neither pay nor receive any Viſits of '''Eſcapade''' or Recreation; {{...}}}}
#* '''1816''', [[w:Walter Scott|Sir Walter Scott]], ''The Antiquary - Volume II'', ch. 9:
#*: [Nobody] stood more confounded than Oldbuck at this sudden '''escapade''' of his nephew. "Is the devil in him," was his first exclamation, "to go to disturb the brute?"
#* '''1918''', [[w:P. G. Wodehouse|P. G. Wodehouse]], ''Piccadilly Jim'', ch. 1:
#*: He is always doing something to make himself notorious. There was that breach-of-promise case, and that fight at the political meeting, and his '''escapades''' at Monte Carlo.
#* '''2011''' March 4, [[w:Richard Corliss|Richard Corliss]], "[http://entertainment.time.com/2011/03/04/the-adjustment-bureau-movie-review/ ''The Adjustment Bureau'']" (film review), ''Time'' <small>(retrieved 23 March 2014)</small>:
#*: He seems on the verge of winning the New York Senate election when the ''New York Post'' runs a photo of David’s exposed butt in a mooning '''escapade''' from his college days.

